    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d341">

                <pb facs="0324" n="317" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg317"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9159: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-03T16:00:00+08:00">August 3, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 4—2:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>362. Our 358, August 3, noon.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d341-f1"/></p>
                <p>1. We learn from reliable source that during Han Fu Chu’s<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d341-f2"/> visit to Nanking he gave National Government satisfactory
                    assurances of his determination to resist any Japanese military activity in
                    Central China and to cooperate fully with Central Government troops. According
                    to this source, he promised the Generalissimo that he would oppose by force any
                    incursion of Japanese troops into the province or the landing of Japanese naval
                    parties at Tsingtao.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, Tokyo. Peiping, Tsingtao and Tsinanfu being informed.
                    Code text to Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
